  "text": "Before You begin: In Ethiopian storytelling tradition, it's customary for children to sit wide-eyed, in front of the storyteller. The storyteller starts by saying, \"Teret! Teret!\" (Which means: \"A story! A Story\") The children reply, \"Ye lam beret!\" (This literally means: \"a cow's pen,\" but when used in this way means that they want told to them as many stories as could fill a cow's pen.) Or sometimes children reply, \"Yemeseret\" (This literally"
